data: {"v":{"message":{"id":"c7099c16-5b54-40a6-ae08-c70d7bfdab00","author":{"role":"tool","name":"web.run","metadata":{"real_author":"tool:web.run"}},"create_time":1793610060.4537818,"update_time":null,"content":{"content_type":"text","parts":[""]},"status":"finished_successfully","end_turn":null,"weight":1.0,"metadata":{"search_result_groups":[{"type":"search_result_group","domain":"www.techradar.com","entries":[{"type":"search_result","url":"https://www.techradar.com/pro/this-ecoflow-delta-3-classic-bundle-is-the-home-backup-battery-id-keep-ready-for-winter-storms-and-its-usd119-off-at-best-buy","title":"This EcoFlow Delta 3 Classic bundle is the home backup battery I'd keep ready for winter storms, and it's $119 off at Best Buy","snippet":"EcoFlow\u2019s Delta 3 Classic bundle is on sale at Best Buy for $479, down $119 (20%) from its $598 list price. The 26.7-pound power station has a 1,024Wh lithium iron phosphate battery rated for 3,000 charge cycles, 1,800W continuous AC output (3,600W peak), and a five-year warranty. It can provide quiet, fume-free backup for essentials such as a refrigerator, router, space heater, or CPAP machine, and is also suited to camping and RV use. The bundle includes a protective bag and a 45W solar panel. However, the panel is too small for rapid recharging and would need more than a full day of ideal sunlight to refill the battery, so it is best treated as a top-up option. The article recommends the deal for dependable outage backup, while noting that the model itself has not been tested; the recommendation draws on EcoFlow\u2019s track record and tests of other products. Buyers should also consider its weight and that it is sold and shipped by a Best Buy marketplace seller.","ref_id":{"turn_index":346008,"ref_type":"news","ref_index":6},"pub_date":1793388756.0,"thumbnail_url":"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/12yuirV-lMZioxxFgvcvVr7sohw5V4nHsH0HVPtZyUp70F8HkuzNIrF9-5j_Xm8lmneHt5Z4P4jHJF80vDPMzN9QrYNEsZF6QplLJQQc1Pm2Jc9J_W3xJEXlAIJ7YcyG","attribution":"www.techradar.com"}]},{"type":"search_result_group","domain":"www.tomsguide.com","entries":[{"type":"search_result","url":"https://www.tomsguide.com/home/be-prepared-for-a-winter-storm-9-products-i-recommend-if-the-power-goes-out","title":"Be prepared for a winter storm: 9 products I recommend if the power goes out","snippet":"The author recommends a compact, affordable set of nine supplies for blackouts lasting more than an hour or two, rather than buying an expensive, bulky emergency kit. Gather items you already own in an easy-to-grab bag or box, and keep non-perishable food in the pantry. The suggested supplies are antibacterial wipes for hygiene and surface cleaning; emergency blankets for warmth; a first-aid kit; battery-powered headlamps for hands-free lighting; camping lanterns to illuminate rooms; collapsible water containers for storing and dispensing water; a NOAA emergency radio with solar and hand-crank charging, flashlight, and siren; an insulated jug to keep water hot or cold for drinks, formula, or cooking; and a power inverter for charging or running small devices, including some medical equipment. The article emphasizes preparing for winter conditions, when a home can quickly become cold without central heating, and advises learning to use powered equipment before an emergency. Prices and sale offers are included for the featured products.","ref_id":{"turn_index":346008,"ref_type":"news","ref_index":7},"pub_date":1793437200.0,"thumbnail_url":"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/5gtTqg86kLpgA9z_rykkBrc2nKewKZmRW24rb3sPgEtAl3CSk4PSu2THX99zSalZAVA7fW36rEvBkxaoQTEp0Rpy8vQzPbuGXZxRPY8mGDLGlD6iehNFIX9Gr2Ifq601","attribution":"www.tomsguide.com"}]},{"type":"search_result_group","domain":"nymag.com","entries":[{"type":"search_result","url":"https://nymag.com/strategist/article/emergency-preparedness-for-reasonable-people.html?utm_campaign=feed-part&utm_medium=social_acct&utm_source=rss","title":"10 Emergency-Preparedness Items for Reasonable People","snippet":"The article argues that emergency preparedness has moved into the mainstream amid concerns about AI-related disruptions, vulnerable infrastructure and supply chains, and governments urging citizens to prepare. While fears may be overstated, experts say reduced public emergency capacity makes practical readiness sensible. Building relationships with neighbors is presented as especially valuable, alongside a compact set of supplies that can also serve for camping. Recommended essentials include a comprehensive first-aid kit and at least seven days of prescription medication; a durable multitool; a hand-crank emergency radio; lighters and matches; a hands-free headlamp; a water filter users know how to operate; backup power, ideally with solar charging; N95 masks for smoke or pathogens; a versatile home fire extinguisher; and a fire-resistant wallet containing copies of important documents and medical information. The article emphasizes useful, space-conscious equipment rather than extreme doomsday stockpiling, and notes that preparedness should include learning practical skills such as starting a fire.","ref_id":{"turn_index":346008,"ref_type":"news","ref_index":8},"pub_date":1793377956.0,"thumbnail_url":"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/ZLEEzeXrGXpWu7_Vmfc477WjsBm0Q-Qp6jil8WBdlN0SUHyDG2aEW-6nNETG31ne8XoSMQrnWWztjmVXEHLlffpGqA5UpHkydITjwGjFalIiFCT_q0LDWU-vp8etU7mwGsbslSbNOi1Q__hue_A31aqF2SLAuYYZA6UhRUzMJ8RviFiuZV4_TOwEasjG6KiX","attribution":"nymag.com"},{"type":"search_result","url":"https://nymag.com/strategist/article/best-emergency-kit-items.html?utm_campaign=feed-part&utm_medium=social_acct&utm_source=rss","title":"The Best Items to Stock for Any Emergency, According to Survivalists","snippet":"The article compiles emergency-preparedness advice from government agencies and 22 survivalists, preppers, and emergency professionals. Its central message is to prepare for peace of mind, keep supplies practical and manageable, and understand personal limits rather than overloading a go-bag. Core recommendations include a backpack or other portable storage; flashlight or headlamp; multi-tool; customized first-aid kit and a month of essential medicines; hand-crank radio; fire-starting tools; rechargeable batteries and power bank; warm bedding; hygiene and cleaning supplies; and familiar, nonperishable food. Store at least one gallon of water per person per day for seven days, plus containers and ways to purify more. Additional gear may include masks, gloves, goggles, emergency blankets, whistle, tarp, duct tape, paracord, cooking equipment, and a tent. Experts stress having a written, practiced household plan, keeping supplies accessible, testing equipment, and protecting important documents. The article also suggests optional items such as a generator, fuel, solar chargers, maps, and Faraday bags. Choose supplies suited to likely hazards, household needs, and the ability to carry or use them.","ref_id":{"turn_index":346008,"ref_type":"news","ref_index":10},"pub_date":1793193780.0,"thumbnail_url":"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/4gfMIeaetH5OPpjqxm8jJhhu0UnCA5XfHp9diG8-0tuFZgihVgauIvJxNiR5VTVg6a8NPoRrzBYoVU5cJv4iLhws5rQU9jj4WeKKErASt_1kC5NLlUpIN9xYdkdS7yL3JrtE13dasqUBMcLOL_1etpvC0wYF3oifOG9u9yftFmY","attribution":"nymag.com"},{"type":"search_result","url":"https://nymag.com/strategist/article/best-emergency-preparedness-items-for-first-time-homeowners.html?utm_campaign=feed-part&utm_medium=social_acct&utm_source=rss","title":"The Emergency-Preparedness Items I'm Buying As a First-Time Homeowner","snippet":"This guide helps first-time homeowners prepare for fires, floods, storms, power outages, evacuations, and other emergencies. Experts recommend maintaining both a portable go bag and a two-week stay-at-home supply kit containing one gallon of water per person per day, nonperishable food, lighting, a radio, first-aid supplies, medications, sanitation items, important documents, and cash. Suggested household equipment includes fire extinguishers and fire blankets, water-leak sensors, a wet/dry vacuum, flood barriers, emergency radios, portable chargers or power stations, lanterns, headlamps, and a properly ventilated generator with carbon-monoxide protection. Evacuation kits should also include N95 masks, prescription medicines, customizable first-aid supplies, and pet necessities such as food, water, leashes, carriers, bowls, and identification photos. For more severe disruptions, the article recommends trauma kits, portable toilets, camping stoves, and water filters or purifiers. Rechargeable devices should carry certification from an OSHA-recognized Nationally Recognized Testing Laboratory because of lithium-ion battery risks. Homeowners should also enable Wireless Emergency Alerts on their phones.","ref_id":{"turn_index":346008,"ref_type":"news","ref_index":16},"pub_date":1787856692.0,"thumbnail_url":"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/ZSt0mUsfcLda9Kzsy_DHcCMdyJqOfdf0UT8TArdiMd-id_Bw12uzm6lRHN7AQylwmi_l7ga9c3chmI3OhsrvI7Y-owe5lzVLlRwywsMujejGpFNsHhWKnrsA-ltm5Ed67WX1ubX9MHlfG0liWlZXuHQtKd5VUGKR4wJPxgxDQ1hDtTfzZJUQUCX4sPSGMilR","attribution":"nymag.com"}]},{"type":"search_result_group","domain":"www.sfgate.com","entries":[{"type":"search_result","url":"https://www.sfgate.com/shopping/article/slug-bluetti-sora-500-solar-panel-review-22485472.php","title":"I tested Bluetti's Sora 500, and it recharged my 2,000Wh power station in a day","snippet":"Bluetti\u2019s Sora 500 portable solar panels deliver impressive power output in a compact, foldable design, making them a strong option for off-grid campers and RV owners. After several weeks of testing, the reviewer found they could fully recharge a 2,000Wh portable power station in a single day, even with scattered cloud cover. On a clear, sunny day with temperatures in the mid-seventies, the panels generated 463 watts, achieving 93% of their rated 500-watt output\u2014well above the typical 75\u201385% efficiency of portable solar panels. Weighing 28.4 pounds, the panels fold into a compact package measuring 22.4 by 17.5 inches and 3.3 inches thick, making them easy to store alongside camping equipment. However, their four-support setup can be frustrating, particularly when assembling the panels alone. Packing them away is considerably easier, taking less than a minute. The panels connect easily to compatible power stations, although adapters may be necessary for other brands. Despite their weight and somewhat cumbersome setup, the Sora 500\u2019s portability and strong performance make it a worthwhile investment for campers, RV travelers, and households seeking reliable emergency backup power.","ref_id":{"turn_index":346008,"ref_type":"news","ref_index":9},"pub_date":1793039411.0,"attribution":"www.sfgate.com"}]},{"type":"search_result_group","domain":"www.wired.com","entries":[{"type":"search_result","url":"https://www.wired.com/review/backup-by-biolite","title":"Review: Backup by Biolite","snippet":"BioLite\u2019s Backup is a modular battery system designed to keep a refrigerator running during outages, offering a less expensive, simpler alternative to whole-house batteries. Its 1,526-Wh Core delivers 1,800 watts (3,000-watt surges); an Extend unit doubles capacity to 3,072 Wh, with additional units allowing up to 10.5 kWh. The reviewer tested the $3,499 two-battery Backup Complete, which BioLite says can power a fridge for up to 60 hours. Installation requires no electrician, but the 40-pound batteries and undersized mounting template made wall installation awkward; it took about 90 minutes rather than the promised 30. Placing the units on top of the fridge is an easier option. Backup switched over instantly during a simulated outage, and its app provides basic battery and power information, but lacks outage alarms, detailed usage logs, and flexible scheduling. The system can also shift refrigerator use to cheaper electricity rates, though the reviewer\u2019s savings were modest\u2014about $1 to $3 monthly. At its high price, its main value is emergency protection for food and the ability to power other devices.","ref_id":{"turn_index":346008,"ref_type":"news","ref_index":11},"pub_date":1793442780.0,"thumbnail_url":"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/sIUEQ4mam6xAuDAP0xbXvDKauW3yqaUoAVkeje1Ie4rQw0K83SwwDp1etl4NIXSTnSCMKPiKH-8Vnsn_q48751dt7Jg1FhCsmuZUuvZT7W7KoT-1LJygYRqsZ3JhGa4u2MBddGW-VczljwcIj7QJIAUm-_re3jW-EGpxvRIx6WcbXtr4tlnEFmCtdUGZFMRNDTvWW9gNwk5fNUVfdGq2PrjQNHd_LeI3KGtxr1VUO_A","attribution":"www.wired.com"},{"type":"search_result","url":"https://www.wired.com/story/jackery-explorer-300-plus-deal-126","title":"Our Favorite Compact Power Station Is $100 Off","snippet":"The Jackery Explorer 300 Plus portable power station is discounted by $100 at Best Buy and B&H, making it a compact option for preparing for power outages or road trips. Its 288Wh battery can recharge phones and tablets multiple times or keep small appliances running for hours. In testing, connected devices charged at full speed, and a router ran successfully from its AC outlet. Ports include one 15W USB-A, two 100W USB-C ports (one also charges the station), a 120V AC outlet rated for up to 300W, and a car-style outlet. For longer outages or more frequent off-grid use, Jackery solar panels are also on sale at Best Buy: the 40W model is $79, down from $130, and the 100W model is $198, down from $299. The article notes that the 300 Plus is best suited to individuals and occasional use, and recommends considering larger-capacity power stations for greater needs.","ref_id":{"turn_index":346008,"ref_type":"news","ref_index":13},"pub_date":1768597817.712,"thumbnail_url":"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/qSKt6Xf385lqEkE2jUQfYyfKZ4mvJansJ-xgY2yNblQhZ82PrU7Hu-vgnFp0t0nbtdt1Sgo_Z0oFlzurNDoyYAcrkHqIqfFQ5SxG-5YzpnEoMrvimmnXSYn0dxfhCc-6C8rdldOiwjfAMQN7nAajfHFiCD8czcngO0KQ2TuMoiVk3KAe09tZ0jMqDAsw4y9lDxOaFJf4sQOBnvpFZZETPw","attribution":"www.wired.c